# Local development

This journey runs the same one-process boot path production uses: an external
PostgreSQL, a one-off owner migration, and a server that serves with only the
two serving logins. Everything lives on your machine and survives restarts.
The next steps up are a [development server on Kubernetes](/self-hosting/kubernetes-development/)
and a [production server on Kubernetes](/self-hosting/kubernetes-production/).

<Aside title="Just want a throwaway server?">

`mise run dev:backend` runs the same migrate-then-serve sequence against a
throwaway Postgres that is **deleted when the server exits**. Set
`WYRD_STORAGE_URL` first. Nothing you create there survives a restart, so use
the steps below for anything you want to keep.

</Aside>

## Prerequisites

- A repository checkout with [`mise`](https://mise.jdx.dev/) (it pins the Rust toolchain).
- Docker, for the local PostgreSQL 16 container.
- `openssl`, for the signing key.

## Environment

| Variable | When | Value in this journey | Source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_DATABASE_URL` for `migrate` | Required, migration only | `postgresql://wyrd_owner:…@127.0.0.1:5432/wyrd` | The database-owner login you create with the container. Pass it only to `wyrd-server migrate`. |
| `WYRD_DATABASE_URL` | Required | `postgresql://wyrd_app:…@127.0.0.1:5432/wyrd` | `wyrd_app` password from the role bootstrap. |
| `WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL` | Required | `postgresql://wyrd_platform_admin:…@127.0.0.1:5432/wyrd` | `wyrd_platform_admin` password from the role bootstrap. |
| `WYRD_STORAGE_URL` | Required | `file://$PWD/.wyrd-dev/storage` | A durable local directory. A development object store such as MinIO also works: see [Storage](/self-hosting/storage/). |
| `WYRD_BIFROST_DATA_DIR` | Recommended | `$PWD/.wyrd-dev/bifrost` | Scribe WAL, staging, and stable node identity. It defaults to `.wyrd/bifrost` under the working directory, so set it to stay independent of where you start the server. |
| `APP_ENV` | Optional | `development` (the default) | Relaxes the production gates below. |
| `WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE` | Optional in development | `$PWD/.wyrd-dev/signing.pem` | Generated below. When unset, development mints an **ephemeral** key on each boot, so access tokens stop verifying after a restart. API keys stay valid either way. |
| `WYRD_TARGET` | Optional | unset (`all`) | One process runs Scribe, Oracle, and Forge. |
| `WYRD_PEER_ADDRESS`, `WYRD_PEER_TLS_DIR` | Not used | unset | Only for [peer mode](/self-hosting/configuration/#peer-mode). The default `all` process makes every Bifrost call in-process and opens no private listener. |

The server binds HTTP on `0.0.0.0:8080` and gRPC on `127.0.0.1:50051`. Clients
derive gRPC from the server URL (same host, port `50051`).

## 1. Durable PostgreSQL and the serving roles

```bash
mkdir -p .wyrd-dev/storage .wyrd-dev/bifrost
docker volume create wyrd-dev-pg
docker run -d --name wyrd-dev-pg -p 127.0.0.1:5432:5432 \
  -e POSTGRES_DB=wyrd -e POSTGRES_USER=wyrd_owner -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=owner-dev-pw \
  -v wyrd-dev-pg:/var/lib/postgresql/data postgres:16

# Once: create wyrd_app and wyrd_platform_admin with their own passwords.
docker exec -i wyrd-dev-pg psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -U wyrd_owner -d wyrd \
  --set app_password=app-dev-pw --set platform_admin_password=platform-dev-pw \
  < crates/wyrd/wyrd-sql/bootstrap/roles.sql
```

The named volume keeps the database across container restarts. Keep the three
passwords in a local, gitignored file or your password manager. See
[Database](/self-hosting/database/) for what each role may do.

## 2. Build, migrate, and generate a stable signing key

```bash
mise exec -- cargo build -p wyrd-server -p wyrd-cli

# The only process that ever receives the owner URL.
WYRD_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://wyrd_owner:owner-dev-pw@127.0.0.1:5432/wyrd" \
  ./target/debug/wyrd-server migrate
# -> Wyrd and Vala schemas are migrated and valid.

openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out .wyrd-dev/signing.pem
```

## 3. Serve

```bash
export APP_ENV=development
export WYRD_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://wyrd_app:app-dev-pw@127.0.0.1:5432/wyrd"
export WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://wyrd_platform_admin:platform-dev-pw@127.0.0.1:5432/wyrd"
export WYRD_STORAGE_URL="file://$PWD/.wyrd-dev/storage"
export WYRD_BIFROST_DATA_DIR="$PWD/.wyrd-dev/bifrost"
export WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE="$PWD/.wyrd-dev/signing.pem"

./target/debug/wyrd-server
```

In a second terminal:

```bash
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz   # -> ok (the process is up)
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/readyz    # 200 once the schema, storage, and Bifrost roles are ready
```

## 4. First-use setup

Run setup once, from a terminal with the same two serving database URLs
exported:

```bash
./target/debug/wyrd-server setup --tenant acme
```

On a fresh database it creates the platform root, prints the root credential
(`wyrd_global_…`), creates tenant `acme` through the running server, and prints
the tenant's `admin_credential: wyrd_sk_…`. Each credential is shown exactly
once and never written to the server log. Store both now. Rerunning setup for
an existing tenant changes nothing and discloses nothing. To create another
tenant after the root exists, export the root credential as
`WYRD_PLATFORM_CREDENTIAL` first.

## 5. An authenticated client request

```bash
export WYRD_SERVER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080
export WYRD_API_KEY=wyrd_sk_…            # the admin_credential from setup

mkdir -p hello && cat > hello/card.yaml <<'YAML'
apiVersion: wyrd/v1
kind: Prompt
metadata: {name: hello-prompt, version: 1.0.0, space: default}
spec: {provider: openai, model: gpt-4o, messages: [hello]}
YAML

./target/debug/wyrd apply hello             # write: register the Card
./target/debug/wyrd list --kind Prompt      # read it back
```

## 6. The whole workflow with the admin key

The setup admin key is enough for every step: it is bound to no Card, so it
can register Cards, call the Gateway, and attribute evidence to any registered
Card in the tenant. You issue no Card key and flush nothing; acknowledged
evidence is queryable at once. With `pip install 'wyrd[otel,gateway]' openai`
and the shell above:

```python
import openai
from opentelemetry.sdk.trace import TracerProvider
from opentelemetry.sdk.trace.export import BatchSpanProcessor
from wyrd import otel
from wyrd.bifrost import Bifrost
from wyrd.cards import CardRef, Cards
from wyrd.client import WyrdClient
from wyrd.gateway import GatewayAuth
from wyrd.state import WyrdState

client = WyrdClient()                       # WYRD_SERVER_URL and WYRD_API_KEY
cards = Cards(client)
cards.register_from_path("assistant.yaml")  # a Service and the Cards it binds
bundle = cards.hydrate(CardRef("Service", "assistant", "1.0.0"), "bundle").destination
state = WyrdState.from_path(bundle, client)
state.start_bifrost()

# The stock OpenAI client asks for a fresh access token on every request.
gateway = openai.OpenAI(
    base_url=f"{client.server_url}/v1",
    api_key="wyrd",
    http_client=openai.DefaultHttpxClient(auth=GatewayAuth(client)),
)
answer = gateway.chat.completions.create(
    model="openai/gpt-4o", messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "hi"}]
)

run = state.run("agent")
run.observe.verify("answer-is-yes", {"answer": "yes"})

traces = TracerProvider()
traces.add_span_processor(BatchSpanProcessor(otel.span_exporter(client)))
with traces.get_tracer("assistant").start_as_current_span("answer") as span:
    span.set_attribute("wyrd.card_ref", "default/Agent/assistant-agent@1.0.0")
    span.set_attribute("wyrd.run_id", run.run_id)
traces.force_flush()
state.shutdown()

Bifrost(client=client).sql(
    "SELECT name, card_uid FROM vala.traces.spans WHERE run_id = $1", [run.run_id]
)
```

The Gateway call needs a provider credential and deployment first; see
[Administer the Gateway](/how-to/administer-the-gateway/). The same workflow runs as a person: after
`wyrd auth login`, unset `WYRD_API_KEY` and `WyrdClient()` uses the saved
login, refreshing it as each access token expires. The Rust (`otel` feature,
`wyrd_sdk::otel`) and TypeScript (`@wyrd/sdk/otel`, `gatewayFetch`) SDKs
provide the same adapters.

## Restart persistence

Stop the server (`Ctrl-C`) and start it again with the same environment. The
Card is still listed, the API key still works, and access tokens issued before
the restart still verify because the signing key is stable. Postgres
persists in the `wyrd-dev-pg` volume; artifacts and Bifrost state persist under
`.wyrd-dev/`. Deleting either one loses that data. Without
`WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE`, only already-issued access tokens are lost on restart;
clients exchange their API key again.

## Failure and recovery

| Symptom | Cause | Recovery |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Boot fails with `WYRD_SQL_503_SCHEMA_NOT_READY` | The schema is unmigrated, from another build, or its policies or grants drifted. | Run step 2's `migrate` with the owner URL, then start again. |
| `migrate` reports a conflict | Another migrator holds the database-wide migration lease. | Wait for it to finish, then rerun. `migrate` is idempotent. |
| `setup` asks for `WYRD_PLATFORM_CREDENTIAL` | The platform root already exists. | Export the stored root credential. If it is lost, run `wyrd-server recover-root` against the same database. |
| Startup names a missing `WYRD_STORAGE_URL` or database URL | A required input is unset. | Export it and start again. |

To start over, stop the server, `docker rm -f wyrd-dev-pg && docker volume rm wyrd-dev-pg`, and delete `.wyrd-dev/`.

## What changes outside development

`APP_ENV=production` requires a real signing key and gRPC reflection off. A
single standalone process may keep a durable `file://` store, but peer-mode
replicas need one shared object store. Continue with
[Development server on Kubernetes](/self-hosting/kubernetes-development/).

